SUMMARY:ART AT THE BARN
DTSTART:20260930T170000
DTEND:20260930T200000
DTSTAMP:20260930T165140Z
UID:dd079d9479cc9b7bcab5296548bf6f@vermont-events
DESCRIPTION:ART@theBARN Art Gallery opening gala Come experience the only 
 art gallery inside a working dairy barn in the entire world!!! Sliding sca
 le entry tickets from $10.00--Tickets available at the door Please join us
  for the opening of an art gallery featuring the original artwork of Steph
 en Leslie. Wednesday September 30th from 5-8PM at the farm. Meet the artis
 t and farmer Stephen Leslie and hob nob with the farm crew. We will have o
 ur Cobb Hill cheeses for snacking and Hillside Herbal Mocktails. Special g
 uest performer local singer-songwriter Jenna Rice will perform a set of he
 r original music. Tickets sliding scale. RSVP & pre-order tickets at cedar
 mountainfarm.org If you can't spare the ten bucks come anyway and bring a 
 friend! Cedar Mountain Farm at Cobb Hill co-housing Ticket price sliding s
 cale beginning at $10\n\nSources: Vermont Public
LOCATION:Vermont Public is independent\, community-supported media\, servi
 ng Vermont with trusted\, relevant and essential information. We share sto
 ries that bring people together\, from every corner of our region.\, © 20
 26 Vermont Public | 365 Troy Ave. Colchester\, VT 05446
URL:https://www.vermontpublic.org/vermont-events-calendar/event/art-at-the
 -barn-08-09-2026-09-15-19
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Rappahannock County
DTSTART:20260930T193000
DTEND:20260930T213000
DTSTAMP:20260930T165140Z
UID:5cf5c20124b243676877f23f8a1657@vermont-events
DESCRIPTION:Our Fall Festival's cornerstone event\, Rappahannock County is
  an operatic tapestry of stories from the American Civil War\, with music 
 by renowned composer Ricky Ian Gordon and a libretto by Pulitzer Prize-win
 ner Mark Campbell. Premiered in 2011 and inspired by diaries\, letters and
  firsthand accounts\, this moving work gives voice to soldiers\, enslaved 
 people\, families\, and civilians whose lives were forever changed by the 
 nation's defining conflict. This moving work premiered in 2011\, inspired 
 by diaries\, letters\, and firsthand accounts\, gives voice to soldiers\, 
 enslaved people\, families\, and civilians whose lives were forever change
 d by the nation's defining conflict. Set in Virginia but resonating far be
 yond its borders\, this opera explores themes of freedom\, loss\, courage
 \, and reconciliation that remain deeply relevant today. With Gordon's lyr
 ical score and Campbell's poignant libretto\, Rappahannock County offers a
 n intimate reflection on the human cost of war and the enduring power of e
 mpathy and hope. Stay for post-show conversations with the cast & creative
  team\, plus composer Ricky Ian Gordon (opening night only!). Prosecco rec
 eption to follow opening night performance Town Hall Theater\n\nSources: V
 ermont Public
LOCATION:Vermont Public is independent\, community-supported media\, servi
 ng Vermont with trusted\, relevant and essential information. We share sto
 ries that bring people together\, from every corner of our region.\, © 20
 26 Vermont Public | 365 Troy Ave. Colchester\, VT 05446
URL:https://www.vermontpublic.org/vermont-events-calendar/event/rappahanno
 ck-county-15-09-2026-13-53-54
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Samara Joy & Her Septet
DTSTART:20260930T193000
DTEND:20260930T213000
DTSTAMP:20260930T165140Z
UID:66e233c32ec69b66ac83b492725e9a@vermont-events
DESCRIPTION:A native of the Bronx\, Samara Joy became entranced by classic
  R&B as a child and cut her teeth as a singer in her church’s gospel cho
 ir. And while her family history is deeply musical—her grandparents helm
 ed the Philadelphia gospel group the Savettes\, and her father\, the music
 ian and songwriter Antonio McLendon\, has produced\, composed and arranged
  his own astounding original work—she didn’t delve into the jazz tradi
 tion until college at SUNY Purchase. During her studies there she won the 
 2019 Sarah Vaughan International Jazz Vocal Competition\, which introduced
  her to the larger jazz scene as a rising star to watch. She was heard\, b
 y audiences and critics alike\, as a masterful interpreter of jazz standar
 ds and a rightful heiress of the sound\, technique and charisma that defin
 ed her jazz heroines—including Vaughan\, Betty Carter\, Abbey Lincoln an
 d Carmen McRae. Joy released her self-titled debut on the Whirlwind label 
 in 2021\, followed a year later by Linger Awhile\, her breakout Verve debu
 t\, of which Down Beat said\, “With this beautiful recording\, a silky-v
 oiced star is born.” The album earned her a Grammy for Best Jazz Vocal A
 lbum in addition to a headline-making win for Best New Artist. A deluxe ed
 ition of the album was released\, as well as the EPA Joyful Holiday\, whic
 h took home Grammys for Best Jazz Vocal Album and Best Jazz Performance in
  2025. Her latest Verve album\, Portrait\, which Joy co-produced with the 
 veteran trumpeter/bandleader and multi-Grammy winner Brian Lynch\, showcas
 es the intimate\, soulful chemistry she’s developed with her touring ban
 d\, earning her an NAACP Image Award in 2025 for Outstanding Jazz Album an
 d the GRAMMY for Best Vocal Jazz Album in 2026.Portrait also spotlights he
 r burgeoning gifts as a lyricist\, in ingenious arrangements that meld her
  thoughtful words with music by Charles Mingus\, Sun Ra and her late mento
 r Barry Harris.\n\nSources: Vermont Public
LOCATION:The Flynn\, 153 Main St.
URL:https://www.vermontpublic.org/vermont-events-calendar/event/samara-joy
 -amp-her-septet-01-04-2026-18-54-47
END:VEVENT
